Social spend now has a durable record. Each run of scripts/social.sh appends one line to phases/social/spend.jsonl on the audit-log branch. Until now it was only an Actions log line, and those expire. The harness tests, cargo test, clippy and fmt all pass, and the commit is in (d3df27f7, "Part of #944"). It only runs locally so far: the first real social run after the push is the first test against the real repo.

What changed in `scripts/social.sh`
• What a row holds: {"ts","phase":"social","day","exit","usage_records":[...]}. If the run left no usage record, the row says "usage":"no_terminal_emit" instead, so a missing record never shows up as a zero.
• When it runs: on both exits, including the API-error exit 1 path. It runs before the safety check and before the commit to main.
• How it writes:
  • It checks with git ls-remote first. If origin has no audit-log branch, it prints one skip line and creates nothing.
  • It fetches only into refs/remotes/origin/audit-log, so it can't collide with evolve.sh's own fetch of the local audit-log branch.
  • It commits in a detached temp worktree, so main's checkout and index are never touched.
  • If the first push is rejected, it retries once (fetch, rebase, push), then removes the worktree.
• Push hook: the push uses --no-verify. The pre-push hook checks scripts/evolve.sh, and that file isn't on audit-log.
• Failures never stop the run: each one prints one stderr line naming the step that failed and returns 0. The script runs under set -euo pipefail, so the code avoids any bare failing command that would kill it.
• Path: phases/ sits next to sessions/, so extract_trajectory.py (which reads only sessions/*/outcome.json) can't mistake it for a session.

Tests (tests/harness_logic.sh, social sink a–f, run against a local bare repo as origin)
• (a) One run gives one row with phase social and the usage record, and the existing branch content is kept.
• (b) Two runs give two rows: appended, not overwritten.
• (c) No usage record gives no_terminal_emit, not a zero.
• (d) With no audit-log branch, it prints the skip line, returns 0, and origin's refs don't change.
• (e) A main checkout with a staged edit and an untracked file has the same status, index and HEAD afterwards.
• (f) I added this one beyond the plan: a hook makes origin refuse the first push, and the retry still lands exactly one row.

I also ran two deliberate-break checks, one at a time, restoring the file after each. Making the append do nothing turned social sink a red, and removing the retry turned social sink f red. Both passed again after restoring (249 passed, 0 failed), and no sabotage marker was left in the file.

